WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.960
The term deep learning refers to training neural networks,

2
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.980
sometimes very large neural networks.

3
00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:08.360
So what exactly is a neural network?

4
00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:12.400
In this video, let's try to give you some of the basic intuitions.

5
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.400
Let's start with a housing price prediction example.

6
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.340
Let's say you have a data set with six houses,

7
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:23.000
so you know the size of the houses in square feet or square meters,

8
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.240
and you know the price of the house and you want to

9
00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:29.440
fit a function to predict the price of house as a function of the size.

10
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.440
So if you're familiar with linear regression,

11
00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:35.220
you might say, well, let's fit a straight line to this data.

12
00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.040
So you know, maybe you get a straight line like that.

13
00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.200
But to be a bit fancier, you might say,

14
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.760
well, we know that prices can never be negative, right?

15
00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.100
So instead of a straight line fit,

16
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.020
which will eventually become negative,

17
00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:51.660
let's bend the curve here, so it just ends up zero here.

18
00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.320
So this thick blue line ends up being,

19
00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.480
you know, your function for predicting

20
00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:59.860
the price of a house as a function of the size.

21
00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:04.480
So it's zero here, and then there's a straight line fit to the right.

22
00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.760
So you can think of this function that you've just fit to

23
00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:11.980
housing prices as a very simple neural network.

24
00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.320
It's almost the simplest possible neural network.

25
00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:15.480
Let me draw it here.

26
00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:19.800
We have as the input to the neural network,

27
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.300
the size of a house, which we want to call x.

28
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:26.240
It goes into this node, this little circle,

29
00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:31.100
and then it outputs the price when we call y.

30
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:38.100
So this little circle, which is a single neuron in a neural network,

31
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.760
implements this function that we drew on the left.

32
00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:47.140
And all that the neuron does is it inputs the size,

33
00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:50.320
computes this linear function, takes a max of zero,

34
00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.100
and then outputs the estimated price.

35
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:56.220
And by the way, in the neural network literature,

36
00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.260
you see this function a lot.

37
00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:00.720
This function which goes at zero at some time,

38
00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.220
and then it takes off as a straight line.

39
00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:07.360
This function is called a ReLU function,

40
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:15.600
which stands for Rectified Linear Unit.

41
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.260
So R-E-L-U.

42
00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.500
And Rectified just means taking a max of zero,

43
00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.720
which is why you get a function shape like this.

44
00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:26.040
You don't need to worry about ReLU units for now,

45
00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:29.900
but it's just something you see again later in this course.

46
00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:33.900
So if this is a single neuron, neural network,

47
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.440
really a tiny little neural network,

48
00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.340
a larger neural network is then formed by taking many of these

49
00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:44.640
single neurons and stacking them together.

50
00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:50.520
So if you think of this neuron as being like a single Lego break,

51
00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:55.360
you then get a bigger neural network by stacking together many of these Lego breaks.

52
00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.360
Let's see an example.

53
00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.340
Let's say that instead of predicting the price of a house just from its size,

54
00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:04.440
you now have other features.

55
00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.580
You know other things about the house,

56
00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:11.140
such as the number of bedrooms, which one of right is pound bedrooms.

57
00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:18.140
And you might think that one of the things that really affects the price of a house is family size.

58
00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:21.260
Right? So you know, can this house fit your family of three,

59
00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.440
or family of four, or family of five?

60
00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:27.740
And it's really based on the size and square feet or square meters and the number of bedrooms

61
00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:32.740
that determines whether or not a house can fit your family's family size.

62
00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:37.740
And then maybe you know the zip codes in different countries.

63
00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:41.740
It's called the postal code, right, of the house.

64
00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:48.620
And the zip code maybe is a feature that tells you, you know, walkability.

65
00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:51.620
So is this neighborhood highly walkable?

66
00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:55.620
You know, can you just walk to the grocery store and walk to school or do you need to drive?

67
00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.620
Some people prefer highly walkable neighborhoods.

68
00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:05.620
And then the zip code as well as, you know, the wealth maybe tells you, right,

69
00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:08.620
certainly in the United States but some other countries as well,

70
00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:12.620
tells you how good is the school quality.

71
00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:19.620
So each of these little circles I'm drawing can be one of those ReLU rectified linear

72
00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:22.620
units or some other slightly non-linear function.

73
00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:26.620
So that based on the size and number of bedrooms, you can estimate the family size,

74
00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:29.620
the zip code, estimate walkability based on zip code and wealth,

75
00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:31.620
you can estimate the school quality.

76
00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:36.620
And then finally, you might think that, well, the way people decide how much they're willing to pay for a house

77
00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:38.620
is they look at the things that really matter to them.

78
00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:42.620
In this case, family size, walkability, and school quality.

79
00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:45.620
And that helps you predict the price.

80
00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:57.620
So in this example, x is all of these four inputs and y is the price you're trying to predict.

81
00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:04.620
And so by stacking together a few of the single neurons or the simple predictors we had from the previous slide,

82
00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.620
we now have a slightly larger neural network.

83
00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:12.240
Part of the magic of a neural network is that when you implement it, you need to give it,

84
00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:19.620
just the input x and the output y for a number of examples in your training set.

85
00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:24.620
And then all these things in the middle, it will figure out by itself.

86
00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:31.620
So what you actually implement is this, where here you have a neural network with four inputs.

87
00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:39.620
So the input features might be the size, number of bedrooms, the zip code or postal code, and the wealth of the neighborhood.

88
00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:49.620
And so given these input features, the job of the neural network will be to predict the price y.

89
00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:56.620
And notice also that each of these circles, these are called hidden units in the neural network,

90
00:05:56.620 --> 00:06:01.620
that each of them takes as inputs, all four input features.

91
00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:07.620
So for example, rather than saying this first node represents family size,

92
00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:12.620
and family size depends only on the features x1 and x2, right?

93
00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:17.620
Instead we're going to say, well, neural network, you decide whatever you want this node to be,

94
00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:20.620
and we'll give you all four input features to compute whatever you want.

95
00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:28.620
So we say that the layers, that is this input layer and this layer in the middle of the neural network are densely connected,

96
00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:33.620
because every input feature is connected to every one of these circles in the middle.

97
00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:38.620
And the remarkable thing about neural networks is that given enough data about x and y,

98
00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:46.620
given enough training examples with both x and y, neural networks are remarkably good at figuring out functions that accurately map from x to y.

99
00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:50.620
So that's a basic neural network.

100
00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:53.620
It turns out that as you build out your own neural networks,

101
00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:58.620
you probably find them to be most useful, most powerful in supervised learning settings,

102
00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:02.620
meaning that you're trying to take an input x and map it to some output y,

103
00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:05.620
like we just saw in the housing price prediction example.

104
00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:09.620
In the next video, let's go over some more examples of supervised learning,

105
00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:15.620
and some examples of where you might find neural networks to be incredibly helpful for your applications as well.

106
00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:16.744
So we hope that this is
